!======================== LbHerwig v3r2 2006-10-31 ==========================
! 2006-10-30 - Gloria Corti
 - Adapt to change of dummy library names in GENSER 1_4_1

! 2006-10-23 - Karl Harrison

  Usage notes for LbHerwig v3r0 and later
  =======================================

  - LbHerwig allows Herwig to be used by itself or in conjunction
    with MC@NLO and/or Jimmy.  MC@NLO can be used only when generating
    events with heavy-flavour production.  Jimmy can be used when
    generating anything except minimum-bias events.  The generator
    package(s) to be used are defined by creating an instance of
    the HerwigProduction tool with a name that includes the package
    name.  Examples of the job-options syntax can be found in
    options/Herwig.opts (Herwig alone), options/HerwigMCatNLO.opts
    (Herwig and MC@NLO), options/HerwigJimmy.opts (Herwig and Jimmy),
    and options/HerwigJimmyMCatNLO.opts (Herwig, MCatNLO and Jimmy).

  - A number of parameters used by Herwig, MC@NLO and Jimmy can
    be set at the level of job options, using the "Commands" option of the
    HerwigProduction tool.  The general syntax is:

    HerwigProduction.Commands( "<parameter> [<element>] <value>" )

    where <parameter> is the name, in lower case, of a variable used
    in Herwig, MC@NLO or Jimmy, <element> is the element considered
    in the case where the variable is a vector, and <value> is the value
    to be assigned.  The available parameter names and their default
    settings, can be found in the HerwigProduction constructor.

  - The Herwig parameter IPROC specifies the type of event to be
    generated.  The list of valid IPROC values can be found in the
    Herwig manual.  Values of interest in LHCb include:

       8000 : Minimum-bias soft interaction
       1704 : cc_bar production
       1705 : bb_bar production
       
    For standard event types generated in LHCb, the user doesn't generally
    need to set the value of IPROC explicitly, as this is done on the
    basis of the LHCb event type:

       [LHCb event type]/10000          IPROC
                 1                       1705
                 2                       1704
             Not 1 or 2                  8000

    For non-standard event types, IPROC should be set via the Commands
    option, for example:

       HerwigProduction.Commands( "iproc 1600" ); // Higgs production
  
  - At initialisation, MC@NLO performs cross section integrations
    corresponding to the input parameter values - a process that
    typically takes 15-30 minutes) - or alternatively may read previously
    calculated integration results from file.  Results files corresponding
    to the LbHerwig defaults for MC@NLO are stored in the package
    PARAM/MCatNLOData, and the default behaviour is to read from these.
    
    If the values of MC@NLO parameters are changed via the Commands
    option, then new integration results are required.  This is done
    by setting the number of iterations for each of the integration steps
    to 10 (compared with the default of 0):

       HerwigMCatNLOProduction.Commands( "it1 10", "it2 10" );

    Results files with prefix "bases" (the integration package used by
    MC@NLO) are produced in the directory when the event generation is
    run.

    To make use of results files produced with non-default values for
    MC@NLO parameters, the easiest solution is to make a copy of
    the MCatNLOData package in your CMT area, and replace the files
    in the data subdirectory with your own.

  Implementation notes for LbHerwig v3r0 and later
  ================================================
  - LbHerwig uses GENSER releases of Herwig (version 6.510.2) and Jimmy
    (version 4.2.2), but currently includes its own copy of MC@NLO
    (version 3.2) for heavy-quark production, src/Lib/mcatnlo.  A number
    of the MC@NLO files have been modified for compatibility with Gauss,
    in particular to allow each MC@NLO event to be processed by Herwig
    before proceeding to the next, to change the mechanism for specifying
    input parameters, and to have a less verbose output.  Fortran files
    modified or added compared with the standard MC@NLO release have suffix
    .F, whereas unmodified files have suffix .f.  An include file
    (mcatnlo_qq.inc) has also been added.
   
